<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 4 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered 1-4 in order. Which exercises get assigned
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Add more by appending another <exercise> below; numbering of the
     existing ones will not shift. -->

<exercises xml:id="ch4-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Before you start, two terms that the book doesn't use but that you
      will see in these exercises (and in a lot of other game theory
      writing).
    </p>

    <p>
      In this chapter, whenever Nature moves first and some player can't
      see that move, we turned the game into a normal form whose entries
      are <em>expected</em> payoffs: one payoff matrix for each of
      Nature's branches, weighted by that branch's probability and added
      up. This normal form is called the
      <term>Bayesian normal form</term> of the game. You have already
      built two of them: the used car matrix in
      <xref ref="read-4-4-matrix" text="title" /> and the Boss Gorilla
      matrix in <xref ref="read-4-5-setup" text="title" />.
    </p>

    <p>
      A <term>Bayesian Nash equilibrium</term> is a Nash equilibrium of
      the Bayesian normal form. Nothing new is involved: you find them
      with best response, exactly as in Chapter 3. For example, when
      <m>p \gt \frac{1}{2}</m>, the Bayesian Nash equilibria of Buying a
      Used Car are <m>(gg, gb)</m> and <m>(bb, bg)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Throughout, Player 1 is the row player and Player 2 is the column
      player, and payoffs are listed in the order (Player 1, Player 2).
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-types-a-b">
    <title>Two Types of Interaction</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Two players, Player 1 and Player 2, are about to interact. The
        interaction can be one of two types, type <m>A</m> or type
        <m>B</m>.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Nature chooses the type: type <m>A</m> with probability
            <m>\frac{3}{4}</m> and type <m>B</m> with probability
            <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Player 1 observes the type and then moves up (<m>U</m>) or
            down (<m>D</m>).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Player 2 observes Player 1's move, but <em>not</em> the type,
            and then moves left (<m>L</m>) or right (<m>R</m>).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure xml:id="fig-hw-types-a-b">
        <caption>
          The game tree, with Player 1's payoff listed first. The two
          dashed curves are Player 2's information sets: the upper one
          joins the two nodes where Player 1 has moved <m>U</m>, and the
          lower one joins the two nodes where Player 1 has moved
          <m>D</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="100%">
          <shortdescription>
            Game tree with Nature moving first, then Player 1, then
            Player 2, and eight terminal payoffs.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A node labeled N for Nature has two branches: a left branch
              for type A with probability three quarters, and a right
              branch for type B with probability one quarter. Each leads
              to a node for Player 1, with branches U and D. Each of those
              four branches leads to a node for Player 2, with branches L
              and R.
            </p>
            <p>
              Reading the eight terminal payoffs from left to right, with
              Player 1's payoff first: under type A, U then L gives 4 and
              4; U then R gives 0 and 5; D then L gives 5 and 0; D then R
              gives 1 and 1. Under type B, U then L gives 6 and 4; U then
              R gives 0 and 0; D then L gives 0 and 0; D then R gives 4
              and 6.
            </p>
            <p>
              One dashed curve joins Player 2's two nodes reached after
              U, and another joins Player 2's two nodes reached after D.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \resizebox{15cm}{!}{%
            \begin{tikzpicture}[
              font=\scriptsize,
              nd/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.3pt}
            ]
            \useasboundingbox (-10.2,0.0) rectangle (10.2,6.7);

            % Nature
            \node[nd] (N) at (0,6) {};
            \node[above=3pt of N] {$N$};

            % Player 1 nodes
            \node[nd] (S1) at (-5,4.3) {};
            \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of S1] {$1$};
            \node[nd] (S2) at (5,4.3) {};
            \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of S2] {$1$};

            \draw (N) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {type $A$} (S1);
            \draw (N) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {type $B$} (S2);
            \node at (-3.3,5.75) {probability $= \tfrac{3}{4}$};
            \node at (3.3,5.75) {probability $= \tfrac{1}{4}$};

            % Player 2 nodes
            \node[nd] (C1) at (-7.5,2.6) {};
            \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of C1] {$2$};
            \node[nd] (C2) at (-2.5,2.6) {};
            \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of C2] {$2$};
            \node[nd] (C3) at (2.5,2.6) {};
            \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of C3] {$2$};
            \node[nd] (C4) at (7.5,2.6) {};
            \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of C4] {$2$};

            \draw (S1) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {$U$} (C1);
            \draw (S1) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {$D$} (C2);
            \draw (S2) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {$U$} (C3);
            \draw (S2) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {$D$} (C4);

            % Terminal payoffs
            \node (T1) at (-8.8,0.8) {$(4,4)$};
            \node (T2) at (-6.2,0.8) {$(0,5)$};
            \node (T3) at (-3.8,0.8) {$(5,0)$};
            \node (T4) at (-1.2,0.8) {$(1,1)$};
            \node (T5) at (1.2,0.8) {$(6,4)$};
            \node (T6) at (3.8,0.8) {$(0,0)$};
            \node (T7) at (6.2,0.8) {$(0,0)$};
            \node (T8) at (8.8,0.8) {$(4,6)$};

            \draw (C1) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$L$} (T1);
            \draw (C1) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$R$} (T2);
            \draw (C2) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$L$} (T3);
            \draw (C2) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$R$} (T4);
            \draw (C3) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$L$} (T5);
            \draw (C3) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$R$} (T6);
            \draw (C4) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$L$} (T7);
            \draw (C4) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$R$} (T8);

            % Information sets
            \draw[dashed] (C1) .. controls (-5.5,3.35) and (0.5,3.35) .. (C3);
            \draw[dashed] (C2) .. controls (0.5,1.85) and (5.5,1.85) .. (C4);
            \end{tikzpicture}
            }
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Each player has four strategies,
        <me>
          S_1 = \{UU, UD, DU, DD\}, \qquad S_2 = \{LL, LR, RL, RR\},
        </me>
        and the two letters mean the following, just as in Buying a Used
        Car:
        <me>
        \text{Player 1: }\;
        \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{his move}\\ \text{if the type is } A}}
        \quad
        \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{his move}\\ \text{if the type is } B}}
        \qquad\qquad
        \text{Player 2: }\;
        \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{her move}\\ \text{if Player 1 moved } U}}
        \quad
        \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{her move}\\ \text{if Player 1 moved } D}}
        </me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-types-a-b-p1-words">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe Player 1's strategy <m>DU</m> in words.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-types-a-b-p2-words">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe Player 2's strategy <m>LR</m> in words.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-types-a-b-profile">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What are the payoffs for the strategy profile <m>(DU, LR)</m>
          when the interaction is of type <m>A</m>? When it is of type
          <m>B</m>? What is the expected payoff to each player?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-types-a-b-normal-form">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Construct the Bayesian normal form of this game by filling in
          the <m>4 \times 4</m> payoff matrix below. One way to organize
          the work: first find the payoff matrix when the type is
          <m>A</m> and the payoff matrix when the type is <m>B</m>, then
          multiply the first by <m>\frac{3}{4}</m>, the second by
          <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>, and add.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-hw-types-a-b">
          <title>The Bayesian normal form, to be completed</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
            <col halign="left" right="minor" />
            <col right="minor" width="15%" />
            <col right="minor" width="15%" />
            <col right="minor" width="15%" />
            <col width="15%" />
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>LL</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>LR</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>RL</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>RR</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>UU</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>UD</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>DU</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Player 1: <m>DD</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-types-a-b-equilibria">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use best response to find all Bayesian Nash equilibria of the
          game.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-beliefs-expected-payoff">
    <title>Beliefs and Expected Payoffs</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following game in normal form.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-beliefs">
        <title>A <m>2 \times 3</m> game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>(2, 4)</cell>
            <cell>(2, -1)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 4)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>y</m></cell>
            <cell>(1, 2)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(5, 2)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        A player usually doesn't know for sure what the other player will
        do, but they can have <term>beliefs</term>: a probability for each
        of the other player's strategies. We write <m>\theta_i</m> for a
        belief about <em>player <m>i</m>'s</em> strategies, so it is held
        by the other player. Here:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\theta_1 = \left(\frac{2}{3}, \frac{1}{3}\right)</m> is
            Player 2's belief about Player 1: Player 2 thinks Player 1
            will play <m>x</m> with probability <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> and
            <m>y</m> with probability <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\theta_2 = \left(\frac{1}{7}, \frac{2}{7}, \frac{4}{7}\right)</m>
            is Player 1's belief about Player 2: Player 1 thinks Player 2
            will play <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> with probabilities
            <m>\frac{1}{7}</m>, <m>\frac{2}{7}</m>, <m>\frac{4}{7}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Given a belief, a player's <term>expected payoff</term> from one of
        their strategies is the expected value from
        <xref ref="read-4-1" text="title" />: weight each payoff by the
        probability the player believes it will happen, and add. For
        example, <m>\pi_1(x, \theta_2)</m> is Player 1's expected payoff
        from playing <m>x</m> if Player 2 behaves according to
        <m>\theta_2</m>. A strategy that gives a player the highest
        expected payoff, given their belief, is a
        <term>best response</term> to that belief.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-beliefs-player1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>\pi_1(x, \theta_2)</m> and <m>\pi_1(y, \theta_2)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-beliefs-player2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>\pi_2(\theta_1, a)</m>, <m>\pi_2(\theta_1, b)</m>, and
          <m>\pi_2(\theta_1, c)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-beliefs-best-response">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your answers to the previous two parts to find Player 1's
          best response(s) to the belief <m>\theta_2</m> and Player 2's
          best response(s) to the belief <m>\theta_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-simple-poker">
    <title>A Simple Poker Game</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Two players play a simple poker game with a deck of only two
        cards, a high card (<m>H</m>) and a low card (<m>L</m>). The deck
        is shuffled and one card is dealt to Player 1, so Player 1 is
        equally likely to get either card. Player 2 does not receive a
        card.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Player 1 looks at his card and then either bids (<m>B</m>) or
            folds (<m>F</m>). If he folds, the game ends: Player 1 gets
            <m>-2</m> and Player 2 gets <m>2</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If Player 1 bids, Player 2 either bids or folds. She knows
            Player 1 has bid, but she does not know his card. If she
            folds, Player 1 gets <m>2</m> and Player 2 gets <m>-2</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If both players bid, Player 1 shows his card. With the high
            card, Player 1 gets <m>4</m> and Player 2 gets <m>-4</m>. With
            the low card, Player 1 gets <m>-4</m> and Player 2 gets
            <m>4</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Player 1's strategies are <m>BB</m>, <m>BF</m>, <m>FB</m>, and
        <m>FF</m>, where the first letter is what he does with the high
        card and the second is what he does with the low card. Player 2
        only makes one decision, so her strategies are just <m>B</m> and
        <m>F</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-tree">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw the game in extensive form. Start with a node for Nature
          that determines which card Player 1 gets, and show Player 2's
          information set.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-normal-form">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Construct the Bayesian normal form of this game by filling in
          the payoff matrix below.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-hw-simple-poker">
          <title>The Bayesian normal form, to be completed</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
            <col halign="left" right="minor" />
            <col right="minor" width="20%" />
            <col width="20%" />
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>B</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>F</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>BB</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>BF</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>FB</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Player 1: <m>FF</m></cell>
              <cell></cell><cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-eliminate">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>FB</m> and <m>FF</m> are both strictly dominated,
          and say which of Player 1's strategies dominates each one.
          Then explain in words why this makes sense: what do these two
          strategies have in common?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-no-equilibrium">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Eliminate <m>FB</m> and <m>FF</m>, and use best response on the
          <m>2 \times 2</m> game that is left to explain why this game has
          no Bayesian Nash equilibria. Why is it enough to look at the
          smaller game?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        In Chapter 5 we will extend the definition of a Nash equilibrium
        to include mixed strategies. We will then see that this game does
        have a Nash equilibrium after all, and the book's One-Card
        Two-Round Poker (Section 5.4) is a longer version of this same
        game.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-boss-gorilla-2">
    <title>The Travails of Boss Gorilla 2</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is Problem 4.7.3 in the book, a variant of
        <xref ref="read-4-5-setup" text="title" />. It is well known that a
        fraction <m>p</m> of gorillas can beat Boss Gorilla in a fight,
        and the remaining fraction <m>1-p</m> will lose to him. But this
        time, when a Visiting Gorilla appears, <em>neither</em> gorilla
        knows which kind of visitor he is. They only know the probability
        <m>p</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Boss Gorilla is Player 1 and Visiting Gorilla is Player 2. The
        payoffs come from Section 4.5, with Boss Gorilla's listed first.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-hw-boss-gorilla-2">
        <caption>
          Boss Gorilla 2. <m>N</m> is Nature, <m>V</m> is Visiting
          Gorilla, and <m>B</m> is Boss Gorilla. The visitor challenges
          (<m>c</m>) or leaves (<m>l</m>), and if challenged, Boss Gorilla
          fights (<m>f</m>) or acquiesces (<m>a</m>). The dashed lines show
          the two information sets: neither gorilla can tell which branch
          Nature took.
        </caption>
        <image width="85%">
          <shortdescription>
            Game tree with Nature moving first, then Visiting Gorilla,
            then Boss Gorilla, with two information sets.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A node labeled N for Nature has two branches. The left
              branch, with probability p, is labeled visitor would win.
              The right branch, with probability 1 minus p, is labeled
              visitor would lose. Each leads to a node for Visiting
              Gorilla. A dashed line joins these two nodes, since the
              visitor does not know which branch Nature took.
            </p>
            <p>
              On the left, Visiting Gorilla can leave, ending the game
              with payoffs 6 and 0, or challenge, leading to a node for
              Boss Gorilla, where fighting gives payoffs negative 1 and
              5, and acquiescing gives 3 and 3. On the right, Visiting
              Gorilla can challenge, leading to a node for Boss Gorilla,
              where fighting gives 5 and negative 1 and acquiescing gives
              3 and 3, or leave, giving 6 and 0. A dashed line joins Boss
              Gorilla's two nodes. Boss Gorilla's payoff is listed first.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \resizebox{13cm}{!}{%
            \begin{tikzpicture}[
              font=\scriptsize,
              nd/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.3pt}
            ]
            \useasboundingbox (-7.6,0.0) rectangle (7.6,6.4);

            % Nature
            \node[nd] (N) at (0,5.6) {};
            \node[above=3pt of N] {$N$};

            % Visiting Gorilla nodes
            \node[nd] (V1) at (-4,4) {};
            \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of V1] {$V$};
            \node[nd] (V2) at (4,4) {};
            \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of V2] {$V$};

            \draw (N) -- (V1);
            \draw (N) -- (V2);
            \node at (-3.6,5.55) {visitor would win};
            \node at (-3.6,5.15) {probability $= p$};
            \node at (3.6,5.55) {visitor would lose};
            \node at (3.6,5.15) {probability $= 1-p$};

            % Boss Gorilla nodes
            \node[nd] (B1) at (-2,2.4) {};
            \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of B1] {$B$};
            \node[nd] (B2) at (2,2.4) {};
            \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of B2] {$B$};

            % Terminal payoffs
            \node (L1) at (-6.2,0.8) {$(6,0)$};
            \node (T1) at (-3.2,0.8) {$(-1,5)$};
            \node (T2) at (-0.8,0.8) {$(3,3)$};
            \node (T3) at (0.8,0.8) {$(5,-1)$};
            \node (T4) at (3.2,0.8) {$(3,3)$};
            \node (L2) at (6.2,0.8) {$(6,0)$};

            \draw (V1) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {$l$} (L1);
            \draw (V1) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {$c$} (B1);
            \draw (V2) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {$c$} (B2);
            \draw (V2) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {$l$} (L2);

            \draw (B1) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$f$} (T1);
            \draw (B1) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$a$} (T2);
            \draw (B2) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$f$} (T3);
            \draw (B2) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$a$} (T4);

            % Information sets
            \draw[dashed] (V1) -- (V2);
            \draw[dashed] (B1) -- (B2);
            \end{tikzpicture}
            }
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Because of the information sets, each gorilla has only two
        strategies. Visiting Gorilla can challenge (<m>c</m>) or leave
        (<m>l</m>), and Boss Gorilla can fight if challenged (<m>f</m>)
        or acquiesce if challenged (<m>a</m>).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-boss-gorilla-2-matrix">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the Bayesian normal form of the game, a <m>2 \times 2</m>
          payoff matrix with entries in terms of <m>p</m>. One way to do
          this is to find the payoff matrix when Visiting Gorilla would
          win and the payoff matrix when he would lose, then multiply the
          first by <m>p</m>, multiply the second by <m>1-p</m>, and add.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-boss-gorilla-2-cases">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use best response to find the Bayesian Nash equilibria in each
          of three cases:
          <m>0 \lt p \lt \frac{1}{6}</m>,
          <m>\frac{1}{6} \lt p \lt \frac{1}{3}</m>, and
          <m>\frac{1}{3} \lt p \lt 1</m>.
          For each equilibrium, describe in a sentence what the gorillas
          do.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-boss-gorilla-2-compare">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In Section 4.5, where the visitor knows whether he would win,
          the game has no Bayesian Nash equilibrium (in pure strategies)
          when <m>p \lt \frac{1}{3}</m>. In this version, where nobody
          knows, you found an equilibrium for every <m>p</m>. Explain in
          words what difference the visitor's knowledge makes. Hint: in
          Section 4.5, what can Boss Gorilla learn from the fact that he
          has been challenged, and what can he learn from it here?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

